Summary

This PR addresses a previously reported security vulnerability related to a misconfigured Content-Security-Policy (CSP) header in the VendorManagementSpa. The original implementation relied on insecure wildcard sources (*), the use of unsafe-inline, and included unused third-party domains (e.g., Google Tag Manager) which introduced potential attack vectors including CSP bypass via known JSONP endpoints.

⸻

Key Changes
	•	Replaced unsafe-inline usage with nonce-based inline script execution using a dynamic CSP_NONCE placeholder.
	•	Removed wildcard subdomain references (e.g., *.mapbox.com, *.googleapis.com) and replaced them with explicit, minimal domain listings (e.g., https://maps.googleapis.com, https://api.mapbox.com).
	•	Eliminated unnecessary dependencies, including removal of Google Tag Manager scripts from the application.
	•	Updated CSP meta tag to support stricter policy rules that improve browser-level script execution enforcement.
	•	Added support for nonce injection via NGINX using sub_filter CSP_NONCE $request_id, ensuring runtime injection of a unique nonce per request.

⸻

Security Impact

This change:
	•	Mitigates CSP bypass risks associated with wildcard sources and JSONP endpoints.
	•	Blocks inline script injection, a common vector in Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.
	•	Improves overall security posture and aligns with OWASP CSP best practices and Google CSP Evaluator recommendations.
	•	Enables fine-grained script trust using per-request nonces tied to the NGINX $request_id.

⸻

Validation Steps
	1.	Verified nonce injection with DevTools (<script nonce="...">) and matching CSP meta directive.
	2.	Confirmed all CSP violations are resolved in console.
	3.	Evaluated final CSP with Google CSP Evaluator — no critical issues flagged.
	4.	Verified that Google Maps APIs load and function as expected.

